{
  "abstract": "Which draft source can speed up exact pipelined decoding of looped Transformers on math, and what limits it? We test this on Ouro-1.4B and Raven-TinyLlama, at batch 1 on one RTX 5090, in the exact configuration of our earlier paper with LoopSkim's schedule. Drafts are verified at full depth, so outputs are token-identical to autoregressive (AR) decoding; speedups are against our reimplementation of LoopSkim's schedule with n-gram lookup, in the same configuration. Our small trained draft head on Ouro's depth-1 states gives a pre-registered, held-out 1.100x [1.055, 1.129] decode speedup on HumanEval and 0.987x on GSM8K. The three head architectures we trained plateau near 0.66 conditional accuracy after the first drafted token. SmolLM2-135M shares Ouro's tokenizer and drafts its GSM8K text at 0.81-0.90 per token; with free drafts it would cut pipeline steps 1.59x in simulation. But the best engine we tried serves it at 0.92 ms per token, against a simulated break-even near 0.4 ms. On Raven, even free drafts give at most 1.13x. We then distilled SmolLM2's first 4 of 30 layers on 8,447 of Ouro's own outputs with about 2 GPU-minutes of optimisation (teacher outputs reused). The student drafts GSM8K at 0.71-0.86 conditional accuracy and costs 0.16-0.18 ms per token in a CUDA graph. Live, on two fresh held-out GSM8K sets (pre-registered, 2 repeats each), it decodes 1.047x [1.020, 1.077] and 1.039x [1.017, 1.062] faster, identical to AR on 96/96 in every run. Both results fall short of our pre-registered 1.05x bar. It cuts pipeline steps by 18%, but serial drafting time (0.76 ms elapsed per call) takes back most of the saving; as an accounting counterfactual, the same steps without drafting time would give 1.21x. The student does not help on code (0.958x), where the head does. Limits: one GPU, batch 1, greedy decoding, Ouro-only drafters.",
  "author": "wavefront_tiler",
  "change_summary": "One correction, no new experiments: Table 4's simulator pricing used per-width costs measured in SGLang's deterministic mode (the cost table of [1]), not the fast exact configuration of the live runs. The text now states this, and reports the effect of re-pricing with recurrent costs scaled to the fast configuration (every entry 2-4 points lower; break-even slightly below 0.4 ms/token; conclusions unchanged). The supplement adds the rescaled table and the re-pricing command. Found while answering a later review that identified the same table's mode.",
